Post-Pleistocene Europe refers to the time after the end of the last Ice Age, approximately 11,700 years ago. During this period, humans began adapting to a changing environment and transitioning from primarily nomadic hunter-gatherer societies to more settled lifestyles. As a result, the food resource base shifted.

Given this historical context, the most prominent feature of the food resource base of post-Pleistocene Europe was:

D. diversity. This is because with the end of the Ice Age, new habitats emerged, supporting a variety of plant and animal species. Humans adapted their diets and hunting practices to exploit this diverse range of resources, moving away from a heavy reliance on megafauna which were largely extinct by this time due to various factors including overhunting and environmental changes.
